.TH SH 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
sh, ash, lash \- the busybox shell
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sh
.RI [ options ]
.B \-c
.I command
.RI [ name
.RI [ argument ...]]
.br
.B sh
.RI [ options ]
.I file
.RI [ argument ...]
.br
.B sh
.RI [ options ]
.RB [ \-s ]
.RI [ argument ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sh
is ash, the shell of busybox.
It is a POSIX shell derived from dash, with many extensions from bash.
It runs commands read from a string, a file, or standard input.
.B ash
and
.B lash
are other names for the same program, and a shell started as
.B lash
is a login shell.
.PP
This page describes the shell as w64devkit builds it for Windows.
It lists the extensions to the POSIX language and the ways the shell
differs from a Unix shell, and does not teach the language.
The applets that the shell runs by name are listed in
.BR busybox (1).
.SS Invocation
With
.BR \-c ,
the shell runs the first operand,
.IR command ,
as a script.
The next operand, if any, becomes
.BR $0 ,
which is otherwise the name that the shell was run as,
and the rest become the positional parameters.
.PP
Otherwise, with a
.I file
operand, the shell runs the commands in that file.
The name of the file, with backslashes changed to slashes, is
.BR $0 ,
and the other operands are the positional parameters.
The file is opened as given: the shell does not search
.B PATH
for it.
.PP
With no operand, or with
.BR \-s ,
the shell reads commands from standard input, and every operand is a
positional parameter.
Given together,
.B \-c
and
.B \-s
run the command and then read standard input.
.PP
The shell is interactive when it is given
.BR \-i ,
or when it reads standard input and both standard input and standard
output are consoles.
An interactive shell prompts, edits input lines (see
.BR "Line editing" ),
keeps a history, reads the file named by
.BR ENV ,
turns on the
.B monitor
option, and returns to the prompt after an error.
Ctrl\-D at an empty line, or the
.B exit
builtin, ends it, except while it has background jobs (see
.BR exit ).
When standard input is not a console, an interactive shell prints the
prompts but reads plain lines, with no editing.
.PP
The shell is a login shell when it is run as
.BR lash ,
or is given
.B \-l
or
.BR \-\-login .
.SS Startup files
A login shell first changes to
.BR HOME ,
or when that is unset or empty, to the user's profile directory,
unless
.B \-d
names another directory.
It then reads these files, skipping any that do not exist:
.IP 1. 4
.I /etc/profile
on the system drive, the drive that holds the Windows system directory,
or on the drive named by
.BR BB_SYSTEMROOT ;
.IP 2. 4
.I etc/profile
in the directory above the one that holds
.IR busybox.exe ,
which in the kit is the kit's own profile; and
.IP 3. 4
.IR $HOME/.profile ,
when
.B HOME
is set.
.PP
An interactive shell, login or not, then reads the file named by
.BR ENV ,
when that is set and not empty.
The value of
.B ENV
is expanded first, as in double quotes, so that it may mention other
variables.
A shell that is neither a login shell nor interactive reads none of
these files.
The shell reads each file itself, as the
.B .\&
builtin does.
With
.BR \-d ,
the shell changes to the directory after it has read the profiles and
before it reads the
.B ENV
file.
.PP
The kit's profile is described in
.BR w64devkit (1).
.SS Language
The shell implements the POSIX shell language.
It has these extensions from bash:
.IP \(bu 2
Functions may be defined with the
.B function
keyword.
The
.B local
builtin makes variables local to a function, and
.B local \-
restores the shell options when the function returns.
.IP \(bu 2
.B [[
.I expression
.B ]]
accepts the operators of
.B test
other than
.B \-a
and
.BR \-o ,
the connectives
.B &&
and
.BR || ,
.BR = ,
.BR == ,
and
.B !=
for pattern comparison, and
.B =~
for a match against an extended regular expression.
Unlike in bash,
.B [[
is a builtin command, so its arguments undergo field splitting and
pathname expansion, and
.BR < ,
.BR > ,
.BR ( ,
and
.B )
must be quoted.
.IP \(bu 2
Quoting with
.BI $\(aq string \(aq\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
Substrings,
.BI ${ name : offset : length }\fR,
and replacement of the first match of a pattern,
.BI ${ name / pattern / replacement }\fR,
or with
.B //
in place of
.B /
of every match.
.IP \(bu 2
The redirections
.BI &> file
and
.BI <<< word\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
Process substitution,
.BI <( command )
and
.BI >( command )\fR.
The shell passes the command a name such as
.BI /dev/fd/ N\fR;
only busybox programs can open such a name.
.IP \(bu 2
The
.B pipefail
option; the
.B source
and
.B let
builtins; and the options
.BR "wait \-n" ,
.BR "read \-d" ,
and
.BR "export \-n" .
.IP \(bu 2
When a command is not found, the shell calls the function
.B command_not_found_handle
with the command name, if the function exists.
.PP
Arithmetic expansion and
.B let
use 64-bit signed integers.
They have the C operators, including
.B **
for exponentiation,
.BR ?: ,
and assignments.
Constants are decimal, octal with a leading
.BR 0 ,
hexadecimal with
.BR 0x ,
or written as
.IB base # digits\fR.
.PP
Not available: arrays,
.BR (( ,
.BR select ,
brace expansion,
.BI ${! name },
case-changing expansions,
.BR declare ,
.BR shopt ,
.BR pushd ,
.BR fg ,
and
.BR bg .
.SS Shell options
Shell options are changed with
.B set \-o
.I name
to turn one on and
.B set +o
.I name
to turn it off.
An option with a letter also has a flag form, such as
.B set \-e
and
.BR "set +e" ;
the letters are in the
.B OPTIONS
section.
.B set \-o
alone prints the state of every option, and
.B set +o
prints commands that restore them.
.B $\-
holds the letters of the options that are on.
.PP
These options have names but no letters:
.TP
.B vi
Edit command lines with vi key bindings.
.TP
.B pipefail
The status of a pipeline is that of the last command in it that failed,
or 0 if none failed.
.TP
.B noconsole
Hide the console window.
Turning the option off shows the window again.
At startup, and at the start of every
.B set
command with arguments, the option is set to whether the window is
hidden or minimized.
.TP
.B noiconify
With
.BR noconsole ,
hide the window instead of minimizing it.
The option is on at startup.
Every
.B set
command with arguments turns it on again before it reads them, so
that minimizing needs
.B noiconify
turned off by the same command that turns
.B noconsole
on, as in
.BR "set +o noiconify \-o noconsole" .
For the same reason,
.B set \-o
and
.B set +o
always list it as on.
.TP
.B nocaseglob
Patterns match file names without regard to case.
Without the option they are case-sensitive, although Windows file
systems are not.
.TP
.B nohiddenglob
Patterns do not match files that have the Windows hidden attribute.
For a match inside another directory, the shell checks the file of the
same name in the current directory instead, so the match is dropped
only when that file exists and is hidden, whatever the match itself is.
.TP
.B nohidsysglob
Like
.BR nohiddenglob ,
but only for files that are both hidden and system files.
.PP
As in Unix, a name that begins with a dot is matched only by a pattern
that begins with a dot.
Tab completion also honors
.B nohiddenglob
and
.BR nohidsysglob .
.SS Command search and execution
The first word of a simple command, after alias expansion, names what
runs.
The shell looks for, in order:
.IP 1. 4
a shell function;
.IP 2. 4
a builtin;
.IP 3. 4
an applet, unless
.B BB_OVERRIDE_APPLETS
excludes it; and
.IP 4. 4
a program found by searching
.BR PATH .
.PP
The
.B command
builtin skips functions.
.B type
shows which of these a name finds; an applet is reported as a
.BR "builtin applet" .
.PP
A word that contains a slash, a backslash, or a drive prefix names the
file to run, and
.B PATH
is not searched.
The names
.IR /bin/name ,
.IR /usr/bin/name ,
.IR /sbin/name ,
and
.I /usr/sbin/name
are special: a file of that name on the current drive is used if it
exists, and otherwise the builtin or applet
.IR name .
No other program is looked for, so
.B #!/bin/sh
and
.B /bin/ls
work.
.PP
The search of
.B PATH
tries each element in order; an empty element is the current
directory.
In each directory the name is tried as given, and then with
.BR .com ,
.BR .exe ,
.BR .sh ,
.BR .bat ,
and
.B .cmd
appended, in that order.
.B PATHEXT
is not used.
A regular file is a program when its name ends in one of those suffixes,
when it begins with
.BR #! ,
or when it is a Windows executable that is not a DLL.
The shell remembers where it found a program until
.B PATH
changes or
.B hash \-r
runs.
.PP
The kind of file decides how it runs:
.IP \(bu 2
A Windows executable starts directly.
Its arguments reach it quoted by the rules of the Microsoft C runtime,
and a program that parses its command line differently may see
something else.
.IP \(bu 2
A
.B .bat
or
.B .cmd
file runs under
.BR cmd.exe ,
which Windows starts for it.
The status is the batch file's exit code.
An empty batch file gives a meaningless status, usually with an error
message.
.IP \(bu 2
A file that begins with
.B #!\&
runs under the interpreter on that line.
The rest of the line, trimmed, is passed to the interpreter as one
argument.
When the last component of the interpreter's name is an applet, that
applet runs the script, whatever the directory, so that
.B #!/bin/sh
and
.B #!/usr/bin/env
work.
Otherwise the shell runs the file named, with an extension added as
for a command, and for an interpreter in one of the four Unix
directories above that does not exist, the program of that name in the
.B PATH
that the shell was started with: a change to
.B PATH
in the shell does not affect this search.
Interpreters may nest four deep.
.IP \(bu 2
A file whose name ends in
.B .sh
and that has no
.B #!\&
line runs under
.BR sh .
.IP \(bu 2
Any other file is not a program, and the shell reports
.B Permission denied
with status 126, or 127 when it found the file through
.BR PATH .
.PP
The shell waits for every foreground program, GUI programs included.
End the command with
.B &
to go on without waiting.
When Windows says that a program needs elevation, the shell tries again
through
.B ShellExecute
with the
.B runas
verb, which shows the elevation prompt.
.PP
An applet is run by busybox itself, so no file with its name needs to
exist and
.B PATH
is not consulted.
The small applets that busybox marks
.B NOFORK
run inside the shell process; see
.B \-\-list\-full
in
.BR busybox (1).
Any other applet runs in another busybox process.
.PP
Each pipeline element, subshell, command substitution, and background
command runs in another busybox process, which receives a copy of the
shell's state.
Windows has no
.BR fork ,
so starting these takes longer than on Unix.
.B exec
does not replace the shell: an applet other than
.B sh
runs in the shell's process, any other program is started and waited
for, and then the shell exits with its status.
.SS Builtin commands
These names are builtins:
.B :
.B .\&
.B [
.B [[
.B alias
.B break
.B cd
.B command
.B continue
.B echo
.B eval
.B exec
.B exit
.B export
.B false
.B getopts
.B hash
.B help
.B history
.B jobs
.B kill
.B let
.B local
.B printf
.B pwd
.B read
.B readonly
.B return
.B set
.B shift
.B source
.B test
.B times
.B title
.B trap
.B true
.B type
.B ulimit
.B umask
.B unalias
.B unset
.B wait
.PP
Those that follow the usual POSIX or bash behavior are not described
here.
The others are:
.TP
\fB.\fR \fIfile\fR [\fIargument\fR ...]
Read commands from
.I file
in the current shell.
.B source
is the same.
A name without a slash or backslash is searched for only in
.BR PATH ,
and is an error if it is not found there.
.TP
\fBcd\fR [\fB\-LP\fR] [\fIdir\fR]
Change the directory, and set
.B PWD
and
.BR OLDPWD .
With no
.IR dir ,
go to
.BR HOME ;
with
.BR \- ,
go to
.B OLDPWD
and print it.
A relative
.I dir
that does not begin with
.B .\&
or
.B ..\&
is looked up through
.B CDPATH
first.
.B \-L
(the default) resolves
.B ..\&
in the path by its text, and
.B \-P
by the directories themselves.
.I dir
may begin with a drive, as in
.IR c:/dir ,
or be
.IR c:dir ,
relative to the current directory of drive
.BR c: ,
or name a UNC share, or begin with a slash for the root of the current
drive.
Without
.BR \-P ,
.B PWD
has forward slashes, an uppercase drive letter, and the capitalization
stored on disk.
.TP
\fBpwd\fR [\fB\-LPa\fR]
Print the directory.
.B \-a
prints the current directory of each drive instead.
.TP
\fBcommand\fR [\fB\-p\fR] \fIname\fR [\fIargument\fR ...]
Run
.I name
without looking for a function.
.B \-p
searches
.B C:/Windows/System32;C:/Windows
in place of
.BR PATH .
.B \-V
describes
.I name
as
.B type
does, and
.B \-v
prints only its file or name, as
.B type \-p
does.
.TP
\fBecho\fR, \fBprintf\fR, \fBtest\fR, \fB[\fR, \fB[[\fR
Run the code of the
.BR echo ,
.BR printf ,
and
.B test
applets; see
.BR echo (1),
.BR printf (1),
and
.BR test (1).
For
.BR [[ ,
see
.BR Language .
.TP
\fBexec\fR [\fB\-a\fR \fIname\fR] [\fIcommand\fR [\fIargument\fR ...]]
Run
.I command
as the shell's last act, with
.I name
as its zero-th argument if
.B \-a
is given; see
.BR "Command search and execution" .
For
.BR sh ,
.BR ash ,
and
.BR lash ,
.I name
instead chooses the applet that runs.
With no command, the redirections apply to the shell.
.TP
\fBexit\fR [\fIstatus\fR]
Leave the shell, running the
.B EXIT
trap.
An interactive shell with background jobs prints
.B You have background jobs.\&
and does not leave, however often
.B exit
is repeated; end the jobs, or
.BR wait ,
first.
End of input is refused the same way, but the 51st in a row leaves.
.TP
\fBhash\fR [\fB\-r\fR] [\fIname\fR ...]
Remember where each
.I name
is found.
With no operands, print the remembered files; with
.BR \-r ,
forget them all.
.TP
.B help
List the builtins and the applets.
.TP
.B history
Print the history of an interactive shell, one command per line, with a
number that starts at 0.
It takes no arguments.
.TP
\fBjobs\fR [\fB\-lp\fR] [\fIjob\fR ...]
List the jobs.
.B \-l
adds the process ID, and
.B \-p
prints only process IDs.
.TP
\fBkill\fR [\fB\-\fR\fIsignal\fR | \fB\-l\fR] \fIprocess\fR ...
Run the code of the
.B kill
applet, which also accepts the job names of
.BR "Job control" .
See
.BR kill (1).
.TP
\fBread\fR [\fIoptions\fR] [\fIname\fR ...]
Read a line from standard input, or from the descriptor of
.BR \-u .
The line goes into the variables, or
.B REPLY
if there are none.
.B \-r
leaves backslashes alone,
.B \-p
prints a prompt, but only when the input is a console,
.B \-s
turns off the echo,
.B \-n
reads at most that many characters,
.B \-d
changes the delimiter,
and
.B \-t
gives a timeout in seconds, with fractions; a timeout returns 142.
In a console Ctrl\-Z at the start of a line is end of input, and Ctrl\-C
interrupts the read.
.TP
\fBtimes\fR
Print the user and system times of the shell and of its children.
.TP
\fBtitle\fR [\fItext\fR]
Set the title of the console window to
.IR text ,
or with no argument print the title.
.TP
\fBtrap\fR [\fIaction\fR \fIsignal\fR ...]
See
.BR "Signals and traps" .
.TP
\fBtype\fR [\fB\-p\fR] \fIname\fR ...
Say how each
.I name
would run.
With any option, print only the file, or the name, as
.B command \-v
does.
.TP
\fBulimit\fR
Accepted, with any arguments, and does nothing; the status is 1.
.TP
\fBumask\fR [\fB\-S\fR] [\fImode\fR]
Set or print the file mode creation mask.
The shell also exports the mask as
.BR BB_UMASK ,
so that busybox programs it starts use the same mask.
.TP
\fBunset\fR [\fB\-fv\fR] \fIname\fR ...
Remove variables, or with
.B \-f
functions.
.TP
\fBwait\fR [\fB\-n\fR] [\fIprocess\fR | \fIjob\fR ...]
Wait for background jobs, for all of them when none is named, or with
.B \-n
for the next one.
The status is that of the last job named, or of the job that
.B \-n
waited for, and 0 when all jobs were waited for.
A process that is not a job gives 127.
With no operands, Ctrl\-C ends the wait with status 130.
.SS Job control
Windows has no process groups or stopped processes, so the shell has no
job control in the Unix sense.
There is no
.B fg
or
.BR bg ,
and Ctrl\-Z does not suspend anything.
These parts exist:
.IP \(bu 2
A command followed by
.B &
runs in the background.
The job ignores Ctrl\-C, and its first process reads from
.IR /dev/null .
.B $!\&
holds its process ID.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR jobs ,
.BR wait ,
and
.B kill
take job names:
.BI % n\fR,
.BR %% ,
.BR %+ ,
.BR %\- ,
.BI % string\fR,
and
.BI %? string\fR.
.BI % string
names the job whose command begins with
.IR string ,
and
.BI %? string
the one whose command contains it; both need
.BR monitor .
.IP \(bu 2
With the
.B monitor
option, which an interactive shell turns on, the shell records the text
of each job.
Before it reads each command at the top level, it also reports on
standard error each job that has finished, with a line such as
.B [1]+ Done
and the command.
Without
.BR monitor ,
.B jobs
shows no command text and nothing is reported.
.IP \(bu 2
An interactive shell prints
.BI [ n "] " pid
when it starts a background job.
.SS Signals and traps
Windows has no signals.
The shell runs an
.B INT
trap when the user presses Ctrl\-C or Ctrl\-Break in its console, an
.B EXIT
trap when it exits, and an
.B ERR
trap when a command fails.
The
.B ERR
trap does not run inside functions unless
.B \-E
is set.
.PP
.B trap
accepts signal names for other signals, and lists them, but they never
run.
.B DEBUG
is not accepted.
.B trap \(aq\(aq INT
makes the shell and the programs it starts ignore Ctrl\-C; any other
action for
.B INT
turns Ctrl\-C on again.
.PP
Windows sends Ctrl\-C to every process attached to the console.
A script that does not trap it is stopped, without running its
.B EXIT
trap.
A program that Ctrl\-C ends has status 130.
.SS Prompts
An interactive shell shows
.B PS1
before a command and
.B PS2
before a continuation line.
.B PS1
starts as
.B \ew\ \e$
followed by a space, and
.B PS2
as
.B >
followed by a space.
Each time, the shell performs parameter expansion, command
substitution, and arithmetic expansion on the value, and then the line
editor handles these backslash sequences:
.TP
.B \ew
The current directory, with the start of
.B HOME
shown as
.BR ~ .
.TP
.B \eW
The last part of the current directory.
.TP
.B \eu
The user name.
.TP
.BR \eh ", " \eH
The host name, up to the first dot for
.BR \eh .
.TP
.B \e$
.B #
when the shell is elevated, which Windows reports as user 0, and
.B $
otherwise.
.TP
.BR \eA ", " \eT ", " \e@ ", " \et
The time, as HH:MM in 24-hour form.
.TP
.BR \ea ", " \eb ", " \ee ", " \ef ", " \en ", " \er ", " \ev ", " \e\e
Bell, backspace, escape, form feed, newline, carriage return, vertical
tab, and backslash.
.TP
\fB\e\fInnn\fR, \fB\ex\fIHH\fR
The character with that octal or hexadecimal code.
.TP
.BR \e[ ", " \e]
Begin and end text that takes no room on the screen, such as a color
sequence.
.PP
Other sequences, such as
.B \e!\&
and
.BR \ej ,
are not supported.
.SS Line editing
When standard input is a console, an interactive shell reads lines with
its own editor, for lines up to about 8190 bytes long.
These keys work:
.TP
.BR Left ", " Right ", " Ctrl\-B ", " Ctrl\-F
Move one character.
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-Left ", " Ctrl\-Right ", " Alt\-B ", " Alt\-F
Move one word.
Alt\-Left and Alt\-Right do the same.
.TP
.BR Home ", " Ctrl\-A
Move to the start of the line.
.TP
.BR End ", " Ctrl\-E
Move to the end of the line.
.TP
.BR Up ", " Down ", " Ctrl\-P ", " Ctrl\-N
Go to the previous or the next history line.
.TP
.BR Backspace ", " Delete
Delete the character before or under the cursor.
.TP
.B Alt\-D
Delete the word after the cursor.
.TP
.B Alt\-Backspace
Delete the word before the cursor.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-W
Delete the blank-delimited word before the cursor.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-K
Delete to the end of the line.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-U
Delete to the start of the line.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-L
Clear the screen and redraw the line.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-R
Search the history backwards for a line that contains what you type.
Ctrl\-R again finds an earlier line, and Backspace edits the search
string.
Any other control or special key ends the search and then acts on the
found line as usual, so Enter runs it and an arrow moves in it.
.TP
.B Tab
Complete the word before the cursor; see
.BR Completion .
.TP
.B Ctrl\-C
Abandon the line and return to the prompt.
After
.BR "trap \(aq\(aq INT" ,
Ctrl\-C does nothing.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-D
Delete the character under the cursor, or at an empty line end the
input, which leaves the shell (see
.B exit
and
.BR \-I ).
.TP
.B Ctrl\-Z
Change the backslashes in the line to forward slashes.
.PP
There is no yank key; only vi mode can put back deleted text.
The editor never beeps.
.PP
With
.BR "set \-o vi" ,
lines are edited with vi keys.
The editor starts each line in insert mode, and Esc enters command
mode.
The command mode keys are:
.IP \(bu 2
.BR h ,
.BR l ,
.BR 0 ,
.BR $ ,
.BR w ,
.BR W ,
.BR b ,
.BR B ,
.BR e ,
and
.B E
to move;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR i ,
.BR I ,
.BR a ,
and
.B A
to insert;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR x ,
.BR X ,
.BR D ,
.BR C ,
.BR r ,
.BR p ,
and
.B P
to change text, where
.B p
and
.B P
put back the text that the last of
.BR x ,
.BR X ,
.BR D ,
.BR C ,
.BR d ,
.BR c ,
Alt\-D, or Alt\-Backspace deleted;
.IP \(bu 2
.B d
and
.B c
followed by
.BR w ,
.BR W ,
.BR b ,
.BR B ,
.BR e ,
.BR E ,
.BR $ ,
or a space, or by themselves for the whole line; and
.IP \(bu 2
.B k
and
.B j
for the previous and next history line.
.SS Completion
Tab completes the word before the cursor.
At the start of a command the candidates are the builtins, functions,
aliases, applets, the programs in
.BR PATH ,
and the subdirectories of the current directory;
after
.B cd
they are directory names, and elsewhere file names.
A word that begins with
.B ~/
starts at
.BR HOME .
File names match without regard to case, but the names of builtins,
functions, aliases, and applets match only in the same case.
A single match is completed, with special characters quoted, and
followed by a space, or by a slash for a directory.
With several, the first Tab completes their common prefix and the next
Tab lists them.
A Tab where a command name would begin, with nothing typed yet, does
nothing, since listing every program in
.B PATH
takes seconds on Windows.
.SS History
An interactive shell with a console keeps the lines it reads, apart from
empty lines and repeats of the previous line.
The lines are in memory, and in the history file.
The shell appends each line to the file as soon as it is entered, so
several shells share it.
When the file holds more than four times as many lines as the shell keeps,
the shell rewrites it with the newest ones.
The file is read at the first prompt.
.PP
The shell keeps 1024 lines unless
.B HISTSIZE
says otherwise, up to 16384.
.B HISTFILE
names the file, by default
.I .ash_history
in the directory in
.BR HOME .
There is no file, and no saving, when
.B HISTFILE
is empty or when it and
.B HOME
are both unset.
The shell reads both variables once, when it finishes the startup
files, so set them in a profile or in the
.B ENV
file.
Up, Down, Ctrl\-P, Ctrl\-N, and Ctrl\-R browse the history, and
.B history
lists it.
There is no history expansion, and no
.BR fc .
.SH OPTIONS
Options come first.
A word that begins with
.B \-
turns options on, and one that begins with
.B +
turns them off.
Letters may be grouped, as in
.BR \-ec .
On the command line,
.BR +c ,
.BR +i ,
.BR +l ,
and
.B +s
act as
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-l ,
and
.BR \-s .
.B \-o
and
.B +o
take the next word as an option name; with none, they list the options.
.B \-d
and
.B \-t
take the next word as their argument.
Options end at the first word that is not an option, or at
.B \-
or
.BR \-\- ,
which the shell removes.
.PP
Besides
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-l ,
.BR \-N ,
and
.BR \-t ,
which only the command line accepts, the letters work with the
.B set
builtin too.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " "\-o allexport"
Export every variable when it is assigned.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " "\-o notify"
Accepted, and ignored.
.TP
.BR \-C ", " "\-o noclobber"
A redirection with
.B >
fails if the file exists.
.B >|
overrides this.
.TP
.B \-c
Run the first operand as the script; see
.BR Invocation .
.TP
.BI "\-d " dir
Change to
.I dir
after reading the profiles and before reading the
.B ENV
file, and in a login shell do not change to
.BR HOME .
.TP
.BR \-E ", " "\-o errtrace"
Run the
.B ERR
trap for failures inside functions too.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " "\-o errexit"
Exit when a command fails, except in a condition of
.BR if ,
.BR while ,
or
.BR until ,
before the last
.B &&
or
.B ||
of a list, or after
.BR ! .
.TP
.BR \-f ", " "\-o noglob"
Do not expand file name patterns.
.TP
.B \-\-help
As the first argument, print a usage message on standard output and
exit with status 0.
.TP
.BR \-I ", " "\-o ignoreeof"
An interactive shell does not leave at end of input, but tells the user
to type
.BR exit .
The 51st end of input in a row leaves anyway.
.TP
.B \-i
Make the shell interactive.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-login
Make the shell a login shell.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " "\-o monitor"
Track jobs as described in
.BR "Job control" .
.TP
.B \-N
Before the shell exits, print
.B Press any key to exit...\&
and wait for a key.
.BR su (1)
passes
.B \-d
and
.B \-t
to the elevated shell that it starts in a new window, and
.B \-N
when it is given
.BR \-N .
.TP
.BR \-n ", " "\-o noexec"
Read and check commands, but do not run them.
.TP
.BI "\-o " name
Turn the named option on, or off with
.BR +o .
The names are in this section and in
.BR "Shell options" .
An unknown name on the command line makes the shell exit at once with
status 0.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " "\-o stdin"
Read commands from standard input; see
.BR Invocation .
Given to
.BR set ,
it has no effect.
.TP
.BI "\-t " title
Set the title of the console window at startup.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " "\-o nounset"
Expanding an unset variable is an error.
A script exits with status 2 when it happens.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " "\-o verbose"
Write each line of input to standard error as it is read.
.TP
.BR \-X ", " "\-o winxp"
Given on the command line, keep backslashes in the variables imported
from the environment, instead of changing them to slashes.
Turned on later, change the slashes in those variables to backslashes,
and turned off, back again.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " "\-o xtrace"
Write each command to standard error before it runs, after the value of
.BR PS4 .
.B BASH_XTRACEFD
may name another descriptor.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The shell reads these variables, and the entries say which of them it
also sets.
It passes the exported ones to the programs it starts.
.TP
.B BASH_XTRACEFD
The number of the descriptor that
.B \-x
writes to, instead of standard error.
.TP
.B BB_OVERRIDE_APPLETS
The applets that the shell runs from
.B PATH
instead of by name.
The value is described in
.BR busybox (1).
Setting it makes the shell forget the programs it remembers.
.TP
.B BB_SYSTEMROOT
The drive, such as
.BR D: ,
to use as the system drive when a login shell looks for
.IR /etc/profile .
.TP
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
How busybox uses the console; see
.BR busybox (1).
An interactive shell applies the mode again before each prompt.
.TP
.B BB_UMASK
Set by the
.B umask
builtin; see
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
.BR BB_CRITICAL_ERROR_DIALOGS ,
.BR BB_SKIP_ANSI_EMULATION ,
and
.B COLUMNS
behave as in
.BR busybox (1).
The shell puts
.BR BB_CRITICAL_ERROR_DIALOGS ,
.BR BB_OVERRIDE_APPLETS ,
.BR BB_SKIP_ANSI_EMULATION ,
.BR BB_SYSTEMROOT ,
and
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
in its own environment as soon as they are exported, and removes them
when they are unset or no longer exported, so that they affect the
shell itself as well as the programs it starts.
.TP
.B CDPATH
Directories that
.B cd
searches for a relative name.
The colon conversion described under
.B PATH
applies.
.TP
.B ENV
A file for an interactive shell to read; see
.BR "Startup files" .
.TP
.BR EPOCHREALTIME ", " EPOCHSECONDS
The time since the Unix epoch, in seconds, with microseconds in the
first.
Each reference reads the clock.
.TP
.B FUNCNAME
The name of the running function.
.TP
.BR HISTFILE ", " HISTSIZE
The history file and the number of lines of history; see
.BR History .
.B HISTFILESIZE
is not used.
An interactive shell sets
.B HISTFILE
when it is unset and
.B HOME
is set.
.TP
.B HOME
The directory that
.B cd
goes to without an argument, that
.B ~
stands for, and that a login shell changes to.
When the shell starts from a native Windows environment and
.B HOME
is unset, the shell sets it to the user's profile directory.
.TP
.B HOSTNAME
The name of the computer.
The shell sets it when it is unset.
.TP
.B IFS
The characters that separate fields.
The default is a space, a tab, and a newline.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
The first of these that is set is checked when the editor reads a line
and when
.BI ${# name }
is expanded.
If it is
.BR C ,
text is single bytes.
Otherwise it is UTF-8 when the ANSI code page is UTF-8.
.TP
.B LINENO
The line number of the command that is running.
.TP
.B MANPATH
Where
.BR man (1)
looks for pages.
The colon conversion described under
.B PATH
applies.
.TP
.BR OLDPWD ", " PWD
The previous and the current directory.
The shell sets them.
At startup it uses an inherited
.B PWD
only if that begins with a slash and names the current directory, so
in practice it takes
.B PWD
from the system.
.TP
.BR OPTARG ", " OPTIND ", " OPTERR
Used by
.BR getopts .
Setting
.B OPTERR
to 0 turns off its messages.
.TP
.B PATH
Where programs are found.
The separator is a semicolon.
When the shell assigns
.BR PATH ,
.BR CDPATH ,
or
.BR MANPATH ,
it changes each colon that does not follow a drive letter to a
semicolon, so that
.B PATH=/a:/b:c:/d
means
.BR "/a;/b;c:/d" .
When the environment has no
.BR PATH ,
it starts as
.BR C:/Windows/System32;C:/Windows .
After
.BR "unset PATH" ,
only the current directory is searched.
.TP
.B PPID
The process ID of the parent.
The shell sets it.
.TP
.BR PS1 ", " PS2 ", " PS4
The prompts and the prefix of
.B \-x
output; see
.BR Prompts .
The defaults are
.B \ew\ \e$
for
.BR PS1 ,
.B >
for
.BR PS2 ,
and
.B +
for
.BR PS4 ,
each followed by a space.
.TP
.B RANDOM
A pseudo-random number, new at each reference.
Assigning a number seeds the generator, and unsetting
.B RANDOM
ends its special behavior.
.TP
.B SHELL
The shell sets it to
.B /bin/sh
when it starts from a native Windows environment and it is unset.
That name means something only to busybox programs.
.TP
.B SHLVL
The nesting level of shells.
Each shell adds 1, and exports it.
.TP
.B TERM_PROGRAM
When it is
.BR mintty ,
a shell that starts from a native Windows environment discards an
inherited
.BR HOME .
.TP
.BR USER ", " LOGNAME
The user name.
A shell that starts from a native Windows environment sets these, and
.B HOME
and
.BR SHELL ,
when they are unset, and always when it is elevated, in which case the
user name is
.BR root .
.TP
.B _
The last argument of the previous command, in an interactive shell.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The status of the shell is that of the last command it ran, or the
argument of
.BR exit .
Statuses of commands run by the shell are:
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Failure; also the status of
.BR ulimit .
.TP
.B 2
A syntax error, an unreadable script, an unknown option letter, a
missing argument to
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-d ,
or
.BR \-t ,
or another error that the shell reports, such as a failed
.BR cd .
.TP
.B 126
The command was found but could not be run.
.TP
.B 127
The command was not found.
.TP
.BI 128+ n
The command was killed by signal
.IR n .
For a Windows program, 139 is the access violation
.B 0xC0000005
and 130 is Ctrl\-C.
For a command that it waits for, the shell prints the name of any
signal other than
.B INT
or
.BR PIPE .
.TP
.B 255
A Windows exit code above 255 whose low byte is 0, such as 256.
.PP
Another exit code above 255 is reduced to its low byte, and the shell
prints a message with the code in hexadecimal.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run a script, tracing each command:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sh \-x build.sh
.EE
.RE
.PP
Give a command string its own name and arguments:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sh \-c \(aqecho "$0 has $# arguments"\(aq prog a b
prog has 2 arguments
.EE
.RE
.PP
Add a directory to
.BR PATH ;
the colons are changed to semicolons, except the one after the drive
letter:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ PATH=c:/tools:$PATH
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run the program named
.B sort
in
.BR PATH ,
instead of the applet:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ BB_OVERRIDE_APPLETS=sort
$ export BB_OVERRIDE_APPLETS
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run a batch file with an argument, and show its exit status:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ./build.bat release; echo $?
.EE
.RE
.PP
Remove a temporary file when the shell exits:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ tmp=$(mktemp)
$ trap \(aqrm \-f "$tmp"\(aq EXIT
.EE
.RE
.PP
Start a job and wait for it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ make \-C src &
$ wait $!
.EE
.RE
.PP
A fragment of
.I $HOME/.profile
that keeps a long history, uses vi keys, and shows a green current
directory in the prompt:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
HISTSIZE=5000
set \-o vi
PS1=\(aq\e[\ee[32m\e]\ew\e[\ee[0m\e] \e$ \(aq
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Paths may use either slash, but a backslash quotes the next character
in the shell, so write forward slashes, or quote the backslashes.
Drive letters, drive-relative names such as
.IR c:dir ,
and UNC names such as
.I //host/share
work.
The shell prints paths with forward slashes.
The file system ignores the case of file names, but patterns match
them case-sensitively unless
.B nocaseglob
is set, and the names of applets, builtins, and variables are
case-sensitive.
Device names and the other path rules of busybox are in
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
The shell starts from a native Windows environment when the environment
has
.BR Path ,
.BR ComSpec ,
or
.B ProgramData
spelled other than in capitals.
It then imports the environment as follows.
Variable names change to upper case.
A name that is not a shell identifier, such as
.BR "ProgramFiles(x86)" ,
is imported under a name with underscores, and the original stays in the
environment of the programs the shell starts.
Backslashes in the values change to slashes, except in
.B COMSPEC
and
.BR SYSTEMROOT ,
and the programs the shell starts see the slashes;
.B \-X
prevents this.
.B "export \-n"
on an imported variable restores the value it had in the environment.
A shell started by another busybox shell does none of this.
.PP
A CR before a LF is removed from scripts, from command strings, and
from unquoted results of expansions.
A CR LF at the end of the output of a command substitution is removed
like a LF.
A UTF-8 byte order mark at the start of a script is not skipped, and
becomes part of the first word.
.PP
Before each prompt the shell sets the console's terminal mode again,
undoing changes that a program made; see
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
in
.BR busybox (1).
When the shell itself is started by a program other than busybox, such
as
.BR cmd.exe ,
the C runtime expands wildcards in its arguments before it sees them;
see
.B BB_GLOBBING
in
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
A command line that is too long for Windows makes a command fail, with
.BR "Argument list too long" .
For libtool, a command that starts with the arguments
.B cmd //c echo
runs with
.B /c
in place of
.BR //c .
.PP
The shell does not use file associations: a file that is not a program
cannot be run by naming it.
.PP
w64devkit changes the shell of busybox-w32 as follows.
A login shell reads the kit's profile from
.I etc
in the directory above the one that holds
.IR busybox.exe ,
rather than from
.I etc
in that directory.
The editor never beeps, and Tab with nothing typed at the start of a
command does nothing.
The history holds 1024 lines by default and 16384 at most.
The
.B noiconify
option starts on, and every
.B set
command with arguments turns it on again, so
.B noconsole
hides the console window instead of minimizing it.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR echo (1),
.BR kill (1),
.BR man (1),
.BR printf (1),
.BR su (1),
.BR test (1),
.BR w64devkit (1)
